e-07,-0.012269614264369011,-0.00016652150952722877],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,1359,18169,24609,17228,315,264,5792,12371,6113,448,12822,400,19,3,14924,1293,11,1378,3232,42939,42446,525,14122,11,438,6839,13,3555,374,279,3082,11,304,9334,14924,11,315,279,5537,429,374,4185,311,279,1378,42446,30,17399,697,4226,304,44548,17855,1352,13,508,6405,921,7633,1188,15,11,18,29621,7,15,11,23,29621,7,19,11,16,16,29621,7,23,11,23,29621,7,23,11,18,29621,7,19,11,15,29621,21621,11,11453,10,57212,7,16,1106,7633,1188,19,11,15,29621,7,15,11,23,29621,7,23,11,23,29621,21621,11,3691,51412,13571,10,57212,7,16,1106,7633,1188,15,11,18,29621,7,19,11,16,16,29621,7,23,11,18,29621,21621,11,3691,51412,15521,10,57212,7,16,1106,1502,445,19,44970,23,11,20,13,20,701,36,317,24157,6405,60,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nBy joining alternate vertices of a regular hexagon with edges $4$ inches long, two equilateral triangles are formed, as shown. What is the area, in square inches, of the region that is common to the two triangles? Express your answer in simplest radical form. [asy]\ndraw((0,3)--(0,8)--(4,11)--(8,8)--(8,3)--(4,0)--cycle,black+linewidth(1));\ndraw((4,0)--(0,8)--(8,8)--cycle, black+dashed+linewidth(1));\ndraw((0,3)--(4,11)--(8,3)--cycle, black+dotted+linewidth(1));\nlabel(\"4\",(8,5.5),E);\n[/asy]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"5b6e4fc7504f83664d0c9030035887b3adcc7132fbd93a6780ac61da635a28c2"},"seconds":25.427446842193604,"seed":340824634181885283,"stop":"eos","text":"To find the area of the region common to the two equilateral triangles formed by joining alternate vertices of a regular hexagon, we follow these steps:\n\n1. **Understanding the Geometry**:\n   - The hexagon has a side length of $4$ inches, and by joining alternate vertices, we form two equilateral triangles. \n   - Each of these triangles shares the same side length as the hexagon, which is $4$ inches.\n   - The region that is common to both triangles is a regular hexagon with side length $2$ inches (half of the side length of the original hexagon).\n\n2. **Area of an Equilateral Triangle**:\n   - The formula for the area of an equilateral triangle with side length $s$ is given by:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Area} = \\frac{s^2 \\sqrt{3}}{4}\n     \\]\n   - For the larger equilateral triangles formed, the side length $s = 4$ inches. Thus, the area of one of these triangles is:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Area} = \\frac{4^2 \\sqrt{3}}{4} = \\frac{16 \\sqrt{3}}{4} = 4\\sqrt{3} \\text{ square inches}\n     \\]\n\n3. **Area of the Common Region**:\n   - The common region is a smaller equilateral triangle with side length $2$ inches.\n   - Using the area formula for an equilateral triangle with side length $s = 2$ inches:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Area} = \\frac{2^2 \\sqrt{3}}{4} = \\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{4} = \\sqrt{3} \\text{ square inches}\n     \\]\n\n4. **Final Answer**:\n   - The area of the region that is common to the two triangles is:\n     \\[\n     \\boxed{\\sqrt{3}}\n     \\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\sqrt{3}}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
